rules/schrybwys.md and rules/schrybwys-compact.md
-------------------------------------------------
Licensed CC BY-SA 4.0; see rules/LICENSE. These describe the codified "schriftsprach-nah" spelling system for Bernese
German. That system is not ours. It was established by:

  Werner Marti, "Bärndütschi Schrybwys" (Francke, Bern, 1978) and
  "Berndeutsch-Grammatik" (Francke, Bern, 1985)

  Otto von Greyerz / Ruth Bietenhard, "Berndeutsches Wörterbuch"
  (10th edition, Cosmos, 2017)

and is summarised for the public by berndeutsch.ch, notably in:

  Ursula Pinheiro-Weber, "Bärndütsch schrybe: schriftsprach-nach" (2021),
  licensed CC BY-ND 4.0
  https://www.berndeutsch.ch/doc/berndeutsch-schreiben-schriftsprach-nah-v1.pdf

  Hans Jürg Zingg, "Bärndütsch schrybe: uussprach-nach" (2021), the
  aussprach-nah counterpart, same licence
  https://www.berndeutsch.ch/doc/berndeutsch-schreiben-aussprach-nah-v1.pdf

  https://www.berndeutsch.ch/pages/schrybe (the free summary page)

The rules a spelling system prescribes are facts about that system, and they
are stated here in our own words and our own arrangement.

About the examples, measured by a script rather than asserted by a human. This
paragraph was written by hand three times and was wrong all three times: it
named words as shared that are not in the PDF at all, it quoted a count that
had drifted after an edit, and it flatly claimed no phrase appeared in both
while four did. So the claim is no longer hand-written.

`scripts/pdf-overlap` fetches the PDF, extracts the illustrative words from
rules/schrybwys.md and reports the overlap, including every shared word
sequence. Run it yourself. As of this writing, abridged only where marked:

    illustrative words in rules/schrybwys.md: 173
    also occurring in the PDF:                73 (42%)

    shared 2-word sequences: 17
    b d
    berndeutsches wörterbuch
    bietenhard berndeutsches
    bärndütsch schrybe
    bärndütschi schrybwys
    cc by
    eugen dieth
    marti bärndütschi
    n i
    pinheiro weber
    ruth bietenhard
    schriftsprach nach
    schrybe schriftsprach
    u l
    ursula pinheiro
    werner marti
    wi ne

    shared 3-word sequences: 6
    bietenhard berndeutsches wörterbuch
    bärndütsch schrybe schriftsprach
    marti bärndütschi schrybwys
    schrybe schriftsprach nach
    ursula pinheiro weber
    werner marti bärndütschi

42% of the illustrative words are shared, and pretending otherwise would be
silly. These are the standard illustrations for these rules, for several rules
they are close to the only ones that exist, and we learned the rules from that
document.

Of the shared sequences, every one of three words or more is a citation: an
author's name or a book title. Among the two-word ones, the remainder are
single letters, and "wi ne" is the linking-n construction the rule is about
rather than a chosen example.

Getting there took work, and each step was found by measurement rather than by
reading. Five example phrases once stood verbatim in both ("wi ne Bär", the
three spellings of "Wi ni gseit ha", "mir luege aa") and are replaced. Several
example lists carried the PDF's ordering, which showed up as shared sequences
("lila d klinik", "stei spile starch", "umespringe verstecke") and are
reordered. One word, "schryne", was simply our typo for "schyne".

What is ours is the prose and the arrangement. The rulebook opens with a
section that document does not have ("What gives a text away"), and the
consonant section is ordered by what each phenomenon is rather than by the
PDF's numbered sequence. Neither document is redistributed with this
repository.

If Ursula Pinheiro-Weber, Hans Jürg Zingg or berndeutsch.ch consider any part
of rules/ to encroach on their work, open an issue and it will be rewritten or
removed. That is a standing offer, not a formality.

The grammar section
-------------------
The description of Bernese grammar (absence of a preterite, the double perfect,
the frequency of the subjunctive, the declension of zwe/zwo/zwöi and drei/drü,
politeness as the second person plural) follows the Alemannic Wikipedia article
"Berndeutsch", licensed CC BY-SA 4.0:

  https://als.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berndeutsch
  Authors: see the article's history page.

corpus/
-------
corpus/sources.md is ours. corpus/texts/ is not committed to this repository;
it is produced locally by scripts/bd-corpus, which downloads Alemannic
Wikipedia articles licensed CC BY-SA 4.0 and writes the attribution into the
header of every file it creates. If you redistribute those texts, CC BY-SA 4.0
applies to them, not MIT.

berndeutsch.ch
--------------
scripts/bdw queries https://www.berndeutsch.ch, a free Bernese-German
dictionary online since 2000 and run as 100% volunteer work by Stephan Burkard.
The site has no API. bdw therefore behaves like a courteous human reader: one
request per page, a delay between requests, a bounded page walk, a User-Agent
that names this repository, and a link back to every entry instead of a copy of
the dictionary. No dictionary content is stored in this repository.

If you find this useful, the dictionary is the reason. Add the words it is
missing: https://www.berndeutsch.ch/pages/wordaddinfo
